raw sushi
sushi dishes featuring uncooked, high-quality fish or seafood, but "sushi" itself isn't always raw, as it's defined by its vinegared rice base, which can also hold cooked ingredients like vegetables, egg, or eel, with sashimi being the specific term for just raw fish slices without rice. Popular types of raw sushi include salmon (sake), tuna (maguro), and yellowtail (hamachi) used in nigiri or maki
For safety, raw fish for sushi needs to be of sushi-grade quality, and farm-raised salmon often has a lower parasite risk, but freezing wild-caught fish is crucial.
